          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "Yes, guidance on using a short-acting reliever alone for asthma does differ among organizations. The Global strategy guidance recommends that an inhaled-corticosteroid-and-formoterol combination be the preferred reliever across different treatment steps. In contrast, some national panels maintain that a short-acting bronchodilator can still be appropriate alongside a regular controller for managing mild asthma. This divergence means it's important to refer to specific guidelines to understand the recommended approach being discussed (source: Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance).\n\nI could not cover individual clinical opinions or nuances behind specific treatment decisions that clinicians may consider when discussing asthma management.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
